---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Sitrep from '../../components/Sitrep.astro';

const pageSchema = [
  {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/vs/obsidian-dataview/#page',
    url: 'https://sitrep.md/vs/obsidian-dataview/',
    name: 'sitrep vs Obsidian + Dataview for AI project status',
    description:
      'An honest comparison of sitrep and Obsidian with Dataview or the built-in Bases for tracking the state of AI coding projects: dashboards over a vault you author and structure, versus a passive state layer over the markdown your agents already generate.',
    isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/#website' },
  },
];

const rows = [
  {
    dim: 'What it is',
    sitrep: 'AI project status dashboard - a passive state layer over existing files',
    other: 'Knowledge base and editor; Dataview adds a query language over your vault, the newer built-in Bases a visual database view',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Cross-project overview',
    sitrep: 'The core feature - status, phases, decisions and staleness across every project, newest activity first',
    other: 'Possible, if you build and maintain the dashboards yourself with DQL queries',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Works on unmodified agent markdown',
    sitrep: 'Yes - parses the organic conventions in plans, analyses and decision docs as agents write them',
    other: 'No - Dataview needs frontmatter, tags or inline fields; Bases is stricter still, reading properties only and no body text',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Setup required',
    sitrep: 'Point it at folders',
    other: 'Create a vault, add files to it, structure notes, write and maintain queries',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Editing',
    sitrep: 'Not an editor - three guarded state operations are the only writes',
    other: 'A full editor; writing is the point',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Mobile and sync',
    sitrep: 'Planned: a read-only, end-to-end-encrypted mirror of parsed state',
    other: 'Mature mobile apps today, with a paid Sync service - Bases dashboards included',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Search',
    sitrep: 'Scoped to the project docs it parses - jump to the decision',
    other: 'Full-text over the whole vault, plus backlinks and graph',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Local-first',
    sitrep: 'Yes - no account, no network, no telemetry by default',
    other: 'Yes - plain markdown on disk',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Availability',
    sitrep: 'In development, launching first on macOS',
    other: 'Mature, free core app, huge plugin ecosystem',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="sitrep vs Obsidian + Dataview - AI project status"
  description="Obsidian with Dataview - or the built-in Bases - is the closest DIY substitute for sitrep, and often the right choice. The difference is authoring: dashboards over a vault you structure with properties, versus a state layer that parses the markdown your AI agents already generate."
  schema={pageSchema}
>
  <Nav />

  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl px-6">
    <header class="flex flex-col gap-4 pt-16 pb-12 sm:pt-20">
      <p class="text-xs font-medium tracking-widest text-accent uppercase">Comparison</p>
      <h1 class="max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
        <Sitrep /> vs Obsidian&nbsp;+&nbsp;Dataview
      </h1>
      <p class="max-w-prose text-lg text-ink-muted">
        Obsidian with the Dataview plugin - or the newer built-in Bases - is
        the closest do-it-yourself substitute for <Sitrep />, and for many people
        it is the right choice. Here is the honest version of when it is, and
        when it is not.
      </p>
      <p class="max-w-prose rounded-md border border-line bg-surface-raised px-4 py-3 text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Disclosure: this page is written by <a href="/about/" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">the maker of sitrep</a>. Read the
        framing with that in mind - and note that where the honest answer is
        "use Obsidian", this page says so.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="difference">
      <h2 id="difference" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The one-sentence difference</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          Obsidian is a knowledge base you author into; with Dataview, it
          rewards that authoring with dashboards you design yourself.
          <Sitrep /> is an AI project status dashboard: a state layer on top of the
          markdown your AI agents already generate - it parses status,
          phases, decisions and due dates out of files nobody structured for
          any tool.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both are local-first markdown tools, which is why they get
          compared. But they answer different questions. Obsidian answers
          "what do I know?" from notes you wrote. <Sitrep /> answers "where do my
          projects stand?" from docs your agents wrote.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="table">
      <h2 id="table" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Side by side</h2>
      <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[40rem] border-collapse text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-line text-left">
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium"><Sitrep /></th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 font-medium">Obsidian + Dataview</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              rows.map(({ dim, sitrep, other }) => (
                <tr class="border-b border-line align-top">
                  <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 text-left font-medium">{dim}</th>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-muted">{sitrep}</td>
                  <td class="py-3 text-ink-muted">{other}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="bases">
      <h2 id="bases" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What about Obsidian Bases?</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          Bases - built into Obsidian since late 2025, mobile included -
          removed the query-writing half of the Dataview tax: no DQL, just a
          visual editor that turns notes into database views. If you were
          only avoiding Obsidian because of the query language, Bases is the
          simpler answer, and this page will not pretend otherwise.
        </p>
        <p>
          What it did not remove is the other half. Bases reads note
          properties - the frontmatter you author - and file metadata, and
          nothing else: no body text, not even the inline fields Dataview
          can read. Point a Base at the markdown Claude Code just wrote and
          you get an empty table, because agents do not write frontmatter.
          <Sitrep />'s whole premise is that half: it parses status, phases and
          decisions out of files nobody structured for any tool - zero
          frontmatter, zero views to configure.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="choose-obsidian">
      <h2 id="choose-obsidian" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">When to choose Obsidian + Dataview</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid max-w-2xl gap-3 text-ink-muted">
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You want a durable knowledge base you fully control - notes, ideas
          and references you author yourself, kept for years.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You enjoy building your own system: dashboards, templates and
          queries tuned exactly to how you think.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You need mobile access and sync today, not when a waitlist clears.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Backlinks, graphs and a plugin ecosystem are features you actually
          use, not clutter.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-muted">
        In that case Obsidian is excellent, and <Sitrep /> will not try to talk
        you out of it - a second brain is on the anti-roadmap, permanently.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="choose-sitrep">
      <h2 id="choose-sitrep" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">When to choose <Sitrep /></h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid max-w-2xl gap-3 text-ink-muted">
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          The markdown piling up on your disk was written by Claude Code,
          Cursor or Codex - not by you - and restructuring it into a vault is
          exactly the work you do not want.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You want project state - what moved, what is stalled, what is
          waiting on you - without authoring frontmatter or writing queries.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You juggle several projects and the pain is re-entry: opening a
          cold project and re-deriving where things stand.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          <span>
            You have tried the Obsidian dashboard route and abandoned it when
            the queries went stale - a pattern common enough that it is one of
            the reasons <Sitrep /> exists.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="both">
      <h2 id="both" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Using both</h2>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-ink-muted">
        The tools compose. Keep Obsidian for the knowledge you author;
        point <Sitrep /> at the folders your agents write into. It never
        asks you to move a file, so there is nothing to migrate and no overlap to
        manage - it is explicitly not a Notion/Obsidian replacement, and that
        refusal is written into its <a href="/principles/" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">product contract</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-faint">
        <Sitrep /> is in development, launching first on macOS. Local-first -
        your files never leave your machine.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
